{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for notes/2026-08-09-connectors.md at exact clean head 5395fdbc6d3e30eeb1eefa2f875de038310cc0f5.\n\nThe revision materially improves the design and resolves the original authority-scope, automatic-reconciliation, and citation errors. The checkout is exact and clean, 0a66e85 is an ancestor, git diff --check passes, the full revised note and the 0178bad9..5395fdbc diff were read, and the cited source was inspected at 0a66e85. Three remaining boundary errors prevent approval.\n\nMUST FIX 1 — charter causality overcorrects into a rule the current act format cannot obey. Lines 180-210 now accurately say staleness follows rests_on and that app.go:734-741 enforces only the static actor allowlist, not charter policy. But lines 185-187 require every durable act a connector originates to rest on the live charter and relevant alias/doorstep claims. Automatic ratification is itself a connector-originated durable act, while fold.go:347-375 requires a ratify act to rest on exactly its target; it cannot also cite the charter, alias, or doorstep. Recast this as a direct requirement for connector-originated root statements and a transitive requirement for constrained acts. For automatic satisfaction, specify that the target report/request chain must already carry the live charter and relevant admission claims. Fold staleness at fold.go:553-580 will then propagate through the chain.\n\nFINDING 2 — resolved. Lines 219-253 limit automatic ratification to reports whose originating request the connector filed. That matches fold.go:359-367: requester identity, not a role, authorizes report satisfaction. The warning is also correct: fold.go:369-373 applies ratifier authority to assertions, proposals, and governance without repository/team scope, and app.go:456-490 plus :495 onward grant/revoke that room-wide role. The note no longer presents the global role as scoped delegation.\n\nMUST FIX 3 — the third command shape records ambiguity but does not yet define recovery. Lines 79-102 correctly identify the foreign crash boundary, and foreign idempotency or marker-plus-readback closes it. But durable intent before effect at lines 96-98 still leaves the recovered connector unable to know whether the effect happened. Lines 374-381 call that durable correlation, which is honest evidence but not a retry decision. State the terminal rule: without foreign idempotency or readback proof, the connector must not retry automatically and must report an unknown/ambiguous outcome for manual reconciliation, or else require one of the first two mechanisms for every automatically recoverable command. The conditional no-private-database claim is otherwise corrected.\n\nFINDING 4 — resolved. Lines 38-45 now distinguish honest participant-authored correction from dishonest automatic reconciliation. Observe and render remain asymmetric; command readback is expressly excluded from the rendering no-readback rule.\n\nMUST FIX 5 — the signature contents are now described correctly but the proposed protocols omit conversation creation. nexus.go:359-360 and :424-440 show that the actor signature covers generation, conversation, sequence, previous hash, and payload; VerifyFrame reconstructs and verifies that body at :475-494. The note correctly says reserve-then-sign can preserve the existing format and detached payload signing would change both format and verifier; only the first is compatible today. It is also fair to call the second analogous to durable submission, where the actor signs an intent without sequence position and the sequencer commits it. However, PublishForSession resolves or creates the conversation at nexus.go:393-407; when no conversation exists, the remote signer cannot know the conversation ID either. The reservation response must include and hold the conversation identity and slot, or the detached protocol needs a separate signed conversation-create/resolve step. Cover that case alongside lease binding, roster check